"""Write, read and review nbdev notebooks as source, documentation, examples and tests. You MUST read this before any work on a project with notebooks.

## Source and reading

Published-library notebooks: interleave implementation, explanation, examples, outputs and failure demonstrations for top-to-bottom reading. Internal projects: match surrounding notebooks.

Check each file, not the repo: `# AUTOGENERATED! DO NOT EDIT!` names the source notebook; `# %% ../nbs/04_usage.ipynb #a45f753a` identifies its source cell. Edit that notebook, never generated modules, `__all__`, `_modidx.py`, or module docstrings; `nbdev-export` regenerates them. Unmarked modules are hand-written. Understand and review work through notebooks and their `git diff`, never generated modules: those lack prose, examples and tests.

Use notebooks to teach APIs through narrative/examples, plain `.py` for dense mechanism iterated under pytest (rarely; never add pytests to a project without them unless asked). Lessons test notebook code; `tests/*.py` test plain modules. For project-level questions, read `nbs/index.ipynb` (README.md's source); regenerate with `nbdev-readme` after changes.

## Authoring

Before notebook edits, read the whole notebook or a summary of every cell. For a new notebook, read a role model such as claudette's `00_core.ipynb` to learn the literate form. Choose tutorial, how-to, explanation, or reference. Open with an H1 title cell, `>` subtitle and brief introduction. Use H2s for major concepts without fragmenting short narratives; each heading gets its own markdown cell for Jupyter collapse.

Develop one idea at a time: small implementation, explanation, lesson for every substantive API. Basic functionality outside the API, with little logic, generally needs no lesson. Introduce lessons in markdown; split when the introduction becomes complicated. Extend examples rather than adding near-duplicates. Build classes with `@patch` beside each method's explanation unless that obscures the API.

When new behavior needs teaching (often unnecessary for trivial logic), choose its narrative position, write the lesson first (better, slightly modify an existing lesson), run preceding cells as needed, see it fail. Add its markdown introduction and implementation above it; run implementation and lesson to see it pass. For bugs, revise an existing example to exercise the failing path; if no revision fits, add an assertion to it. No documented behavior change: change no lesson cells. Never add a cell merely to witness a fix.

Read existing examples before experimenting. Explore in the notebook, not a separate kernel check; keep useful explorations as lessons, delete the rest. Always try to leave the notebook better. Reserve underscore names for machinery without a coherent independent contract, not everything used internally. A helper useful only internally often signals awkward design.

## Lessons

Readability comes first: guide readers through what code does using code and displayed output. If a displayed value is worth testing, assign, test, then show it. Test the point of the code, not incidentals such as formatting. Show errors only when readers are likely to hit them.

Use realistic values, minimal setup, reused objects and direct supporting assertions. Show evidence with compact `_repr_markdown_`, structured summaries, plots, tables, images or HTML; explain what matters in large outputs and keep stored results focused. Use `fastcore.test` assertions and `expect_fail` in ordinary code cells.

Test-serving cells don't belong on the page: signs include safety-only setup and glue checks. Usually remove them; move substantive logic checks to `#| hide` or pytest. Discuss such changes carefully. Keep plumbing, mocks, dense comprehensions and long setup out of reader-facing examples: extract a small helper or hide the check.

Consider these rewrites:
- Commented examples -> prose/code; numbered steps -> markdown/code pairs. Keep comments stating constraints code cannot show.
- Sentence-wrapped `print` -> prose/displayed value; final `print(x)` -> bare `x`.
- Reassignment stages and blank-line groups -> separate cells, each introduced.
- Near-duplicates: explain the differing argument; don't repeat setup.
- Errors: `expect_fail`, not `try`/`except`. Long docstrings -> markdown.
- Resolve TODO/FIXME example comments or move them to issues.

## Prose and exports

In markdown, explain rationale, distinctions and guarantees, not code line-by-line or filler transitions. Place it for correct generated-page reading, normally after definitions. Use short symbol docstrings, parameter/return docments beside signatures and backticked symbols for nbdev links. Extended explanation, examples and warnings belong in markdown.

`nbdev-export` assembles module docstrings from the title's `>` description and exported markdown after H1; use one exported markdown cell per major section for long docstrings. `doc(module)` lists functions; in module prose, explain only shared constraints/workflows.

`#| exportd` includes a code cell as fenced docstring source, not module code. Use compact runnable demos after required definitions; end the preceding exported summary with a colon. Intervening unexported definitions don't appear in the docstring. Check claims by running code before documenting; preview assembly with `nbdev.export.nb_mdoc`. A docstring beyond the default summary makes `nbdev-export` list the module in package docs and `llms.txt`. Only extend docstrings of modules read independently. Export `index.ipynb`'s opening paragraphs for the package intro.

## State and execution

Define values near first use, introduce shared setup, reuse established objects; don't reassign names later cells depend on. End explorations with the expression recording what was learned when useful. Keep imports in dedicated cells, lessons included: docs builds execute import-containing cells in fresh namespaces; mixed state-dependent code can fail or run at documentation time.

Outputs are generated and may be stale during edits. Don't stop work, clear them manually, or edit notebook JSON because of source/output mismatch. At PR time, regenerate with `nbdev-test --save` if the project keeps saved outputs. `nbdev-test --file-re` runs several named notebooks in parallel.

## Directives and nbdev v3

- `#| default_exp` names the generated module; `#| export` includes a cell there. Exported underscore helpers need not be public.
- `#| hide` hides distracting necessities from the page.
- `#| eval: false`: code that must not run, not broken code. Later cells can't use its state.
- Metadata directives work too: `{"nbdev": {"export": "true"}}`; `fastcore.nbio.mk_cell(...).directives` merges both forms, content winning conflicts.

nbdev v3 (Jan 2026): `pyproject.toml`, not `settings.ini`; metadata in `[project]`, settings in `[tool.nbdev]` (defaults `nbs_path='nbs'`, `doc_path='_docs'`), version in `__init__.py` via `dynamic = ["version"]`, `_modidx` in `[project.entry-points.nbdev]`. CLIs use hyphens (`nbdev-export`, `nbdev-test`); Python names retain underscores. GitHub actions: `fastai/workflows/nbdev3-ci`, `quarto-ghp3`.

## Finish

Revise affected prose, displays and assertions together; changed assertions must fail on old code and pass on new. Re-read touched narrative for readability. Run `nbdev-test <nb>`.
"""
